  "response_text": "System calls give userspace programs the ability to ask the kernel to do something. However, the kernel might refuse depending on what the system call is. A system call is a function that is usually accessed by an interrupt but the function does not get compiled into the user\u2019s program code. It is fixed in memory.\\nWhen a system call is triggered, the system call handler is called in to provide a kernel service. The system call handler is always at the same place in memory hence the mechanism by which we trigger a system call just needs to know the memory location and then call the function pointer stored there.\\n",
